  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-ortomaculatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species remains small by Leporinus standards. The largest specimen known to date was 11 cm long, so it can be assumed that the fish will hardly become longer than 15 cm in the aquarium. Leporinus are long-lived fish and will certainly become much older and thus larger in the aquarium than in the wild.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-ortomaculatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Be careful not to confuse Leporinus ortomaculatus with L. octomaculatus, because this is a completely different species.</p><p>It was scientifically described only in 2000.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-ortomaculatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>So far Leporinus ortomaculatus is known from the Rio Surumu, Roraima, Brazil and from the rivers Rio Caroni, Rio Caura and Orinoco to the Delta Amacuro in Venezuela. The animals offered originate from Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus-ortomaculatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Leporinus ortomaculatus is among the most peaceful of all Leporinus species. Although there is constant teasing and chasing among them, as is typical for Leporinus, there is not even any fin damage.</p>"
    }
  ],
